(Ninety older adults \(mean age 67 years\) undergoing total hip replacement were randomized) Tj
T*
(to receive either an enhanced recovery nursing protocol \(ERAS, n = 45\) or standard) Tj
T*
(postoperative care \(n = 45\). The ERAS group received optimized pain management, early) Tj
T*
(mobilization, catheter removal protocols, nutritional support, and other multimodal) Tj
T*
(interventions. The ERAS group demonstrated earlier return of gastrointestinal function) Tj
T*
(\(mean 1.2 days vs 2.1 days, p = 0.004\), earlier independent ambulation \(2.4 vs 3.8 days, p) Tj
T*
(= 0.002\), and faster catheter removal \(1.8 vs 2.9 days, p = 0.008\). Pain scores were) Tj
T*
(significantly lower in the ERAS group across all postoperative timepoints \(mean reduction) Tj
T*
(of 1.8 points on 10-point scale, p < 0.001\). Hospital length of stay was reduced by) Tj
T*
(approximately 1.5 days in the ERAS group \(5.2 vs 6.7 days, p = 0.009\). Functional hip) Tj
T*
(scores at discharge were higher in the ERAS group \(mean 58 vs 51 points, p = 0.03\).) Tj
T*
(Postoperative complication rates were lower in the ERAS group \(18% vs 31%, p = 0.08\).) Tj
T*
(Patient satisfaction ratings were significantly higher in the ERAS group \(mean 8.6/10 vs) Tj
T*
(7.2/10\). These findings demonstrate that structured nursing protocols substantially) Tj
T*
(improve recovery trajectory and reduce hospitalization burden in older hip replacement) Tj
